\title{Bergman Fans as Tropical Linear Spaces:\\Circuit Constraints, Cone Symmetries, and Component Lineality}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 1, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $M$ be a matroid on a ground set $E$, and let a weight be a function $w:E\to\R$. The Bergman fan $B(M)$ is the set of weights for which the minimum on every circuit of $M$ is attained by at least two distinct elements. We identify $B(M)$ with the tropical linear space cut out by the circuit ideal. We establish invariance under adding a constant to all coordinates and closure under multiplication by a nonnegative scalar. In particular, the same conclusion holds for nested matroids. We characterize the Bergman lineality space---weights constant on every circuit---as exactly the functions constant on circuit components. Consequently, a circuit-connected matroid has only constant lineality on its ground set. Finally, every common-translation orbit is convex and path connected, and the orbit through any Bergman weight remains inside the Bergman fan. Algorithms for finite circuit presentations make these constructions explicit.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A matroid extracts the notion of dependence from linear algebra and graph theory. Its circuits are the minimally dependent subsets of a ground set. Tropical geometry replaces ordinary addition by minimization and detects a tropical hypersurface where the minimum among its terms is attained more than once. Combining these ideas, each matroid circuit supplies one tropical equation, and the simultaneous solution set is the Bergman fan.

The central equality is conceptually simple: the combinatorially defined Bergman fan is precisely the tropical linear space of the circuit ideal. The repeated-minimum formulation makes two universal symmetries transparent. Common translation $w\mapsto w+c\bone$ preserves every comparison, while nonnegative dilation $w\mapsto aw$ preserves order and equality. Circuit overlap also determines lineality. If a weight is constant on each circuit, equality propagates through chains of circuit relations, yielding a characterization in terms of circuit components. Finally, the common-translation orbit of any weight is an affine line; when the initial weight is in the Bergman fan, the whole orbit remains there.

The scope is precise. The tropical cone property used below consists of translation invariance and nonnegative dilation closure. It does not claim ordinary convexity of the full Bergman fan. Likewise, the nested-matroid conclusion is a specialization of a universal theorem; stronger assertions about projectivized convexity remain open here.

\section{Matroidal and tropical preliminaries}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{matroid} $M$ consists of a ground set $E$ and a family of independent subsets satisfying the hereditary and exchange axioms. A \emph{circuit} is a minimally dependent subset. Write $\cC(M)$ for the circuit family.
\end{definition}

For a vector configuration, circuits support minimal linear relations. For a graph, the circuits of its graphic matroid are the edge sets of simple cycles. The closure $\operatorname{cl}_M(X)$ consists of elements whose addition to $X$ does not increase rank; a \emph{flat} is a set $F$ with $\operatorname{cl}_M(F)=F$.

\begin{definition}
A subset $X\subseteq E$ is \emph{cyclic} if every element of $X$ belongs to a circuit contained in $X$. A \emph{cyclic flat} is both a flat and cyclic. The matroid is \emph{nested} when its cyclic flats form a chain: for any cyclic flats $F,G$, either $F\subseteq G$ or $G\subseteq F$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Let $w:E\to\R$ and $C\subseteq E$. The minimum of $w$ on $C$ is \emph{attained at least twice} if there are distinct $e,f\in C$ such that
\[
w_e=w_f\leq w_x\qquad\text{for every }x\in C.
\]
The \emph{Bergman fan} is
\[
B(M)=\{w\in\R^E:\text{every circuit minimum is attained at least twice}\}.
\]
\end{definition}

In the min-plus convention, tropical addition is $a\oplus b=\min(a,b)$ and tropical multiplication is $a\odot b=a+b$. A circuit $C$ determines the coefficient-free tropical polynomial
\[
p_C(w)=\bigoplus_{e\in C}w_e=\min_{e\in C}w_e.
\]
Its tropical zero set is the corner locus where at least two terms attain the minimum. Represent the circuit ideal by its family of supports $\cC(M)$. For any family $\mathcal I$ of subsets of $E$, define
\[
V_{\trop}(\mathcal I)=\{w\in\R^E:\text{every }C\in\mathcal I
\text{ has at least two minimizers}\}.
\]

\section{The circuit ideal and the Bergman fan}

\begin{theorem}[Bergman fan--tropical linear space equality]
For every matroid $M$,
\[
B(M)=V_{\trop}(\cC(M)).
\]
Thus the Bergman fan is exactly the tropical linear space cut out by the circuit ideal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A weight lies in $B(M)$ precisely when every circuit $C\in\cC(M)$ has at least two distinct minimizers. Membership in $V_{\trop}(\cC(M))$ requires the identical condition for every member of the circuit family. Hence the two sets coincide.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[The tropical line]
Let $M$ be the rank-two uniform matroid on $E=\{1,2,3\}$. Its only circuit is $E$, so
\[
B(M)=\{w\in\R^3:\min(w_1,w_2,w_3)\text{ is attained at least twice}\}.
\]
It contains $(0,0,2)$, $(0,3,0)$, and $(4,1,1)$, but not $(0,1,2)$. Modulo common translation, this is the standard three-rayed tropical line. The fan is not ordinarily convex: $u=(0,0,2)$ and $v=(0,2,0)$ belong to it, but $(u+v)/2=(0,1,1)$ does not.
\end{example}

\section{Tropical cone symmetries}

\begin{lemma}[Translation preserves repeated minima]
If the minimum of $w$ on $C$ is attained at least twice, then for every $c\in\R$ the minimum of $w+c\bone$ on $C$ is attained at least twice.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Choose distinct minimizers $e,f$. Adding $c$ preserves each inequality $w_e\leq w_x$ and the equality $w_e=w_f$, so the same pair remains minimizing.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nonnegative dilation preserves repeated minima]
If the minimum of $w$ on $C$ is attained at least twice and $a\geq0$, then the minimum of $aw$ on $C$ is attained at least twice.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Multiplication by $a\geq0$ preserves the inequalities from a minimizing pair and preserves their equality. When $a=0$, all coordinates agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
A set $S\subseteq\R^E$ is a \emph{tropical linear cone} if
\[
w+c\bone\in S\Longleftrightarrow w\in S
\]
for every $w$ and $c\in\R$, and if $w\in S$, $a\geq0$ imply $aw\in S$. This definition does not require closure under ordinary addition.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Universal tropical cone structure]
For every matroid $M$, the Bergman fan $B(M)$ is a tropical linear cone.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply translation preservation to every circuit. The reverse implication follows by translating by $-c$. Apply nonnegative-dilation preservation to every circuit for the second condition.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nested matroids]
If $M$ is nested, then $B(M)$ is a tropical linear cone.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The universal theorem applies to every matroid, hence in particular to nested matroids.
\end{proof}

Nestedness is not needed for this particular conclusion. No assertion of ordinary convexity follows from it.

\section{Circuit components and lineality}

\begin{definition}
Elements $e,f\in E$ are \emph{circuit-adjacent} if one circuit contains both. They are in the same \emph{circuit component} if a finite chain $e=e_0,e_1,\dots,e_k=f$ exists in which consecutive elements are circuit-adjacent. The matroid is \emph{circuit-connected} when $E$ is nonempty and every pair of ground elements is in the same circuit component.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{Bergman lineality space} is
\[
L(M)=\{v\in\R^E:v_e=v_f\text{ whenever }e,f
\text{ belong to a common circuit}\}.
\]
Equivalently, its weights are constant on each circuit.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Component characterization of lineality]
A weight $v:E\to\R$ lies in $L(M)$ if and only if it is constant on every circuit component. Explicitly,
\[
v\in L(M)\Longleftrightarrow
(e\text{ and }f\text{ in the same circuit component}\Rightarrow v_e=v_f).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $v\in L(M)$ and $e=e_0,\ldots,e_k=f$ is a chain of circuit adjacencies, circuitwise constancy gives
\[
v_{e_0}=v_{e_1}=\cdots=v_{e_k}.
\]
Conversely, two elements in a common circuit are adjacent and therefore in the same component, so componentwise constancy implies circuitwise constancy.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Connected lineality is constant]
If $M$ is circuit-connected and $v\in L(M)$, then $v$ is constant on $E$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Every two elements of $E$ lie in one component, so the preceding theorem equates their values.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Constant lineality]
For every $c\in\R$, the constant weight $v_e=c$ belongs to $L(M)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every two coordinates of a constant function agree.
\end{proof}

Thus for a circuit-connected matroid, lineality weights on the ground set are exactly constants. For several components, a separate constant may be assigned to each component because no circuit compares different components.

\section{Translation orbits and topology}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{translation orbit} through $w$ is
\[
\mathcal O(w)=\{w+c\bone:c\in\R\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Convexity of translation orbits]
For every weight $w$, the orbit $\mathcal O(w)$ is convex in $\R^E$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $a,b\geq0$ with $a+b=1$,
\[
a(w+c_1\bone)+b(w+c_2\bone)
=w+(ac_1+bc_2)\bone,
\]
which lies in the same orbit.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Path connectedness]
Every translation orbit $\mathcal O(w)$ is path connected.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
It is nonempty and convex. Explicitly, $w+c_1\bone$ and $w+c_2\bone$ are joined by
\[
\gamma(t)=w+((1-t)c_1+tc_2)\bone,\qquad 0\leq t\leq1.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bergman translation orbits stay in the fan]
If $w\in B(M)$, then $\mathcal O(w)\subseteq B(M)$. Consequently every Bergman weight lies on a convex, path-connected translation orbit contained in the fan.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every orbit point is $w+c\bone$, and common translation preserves the repeated minimum on every circuit. Convexity and path connectedness were established above.
\end{proof}

This does not assert that the whole fan is path connected. It identifies a canonical connected subset through each point. Quotienting by these orbits gives tropical projectivization.

\section{Finite algorithms and examples}

Suppose $E=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$ and circuits $C_1,\ldots,C_m$ are explicit. Let $L=\sum_j|C_j|$ be the total circuit-incidence size.

\subsection{Membership}
For each $C_j$, compute $\mu_j=\min_{e\in C_j}w_e$ and count the elements with $w_e=\mu_j$. Accept exactly when every count is at least two. The running time is $O(L)$ and auxiliary space can be $O(1)$ beyond the input. By the main equality, this decides both Bergman-fan membership and membership in the tropical linear space of the circuit ideal.

\subsection{Components and lineality}
Construct a disjoint-set union structure on $E$. For each circuit $C$, choose a representative $r\in C$ and union $r$ with every other element of $C$. These $|C|-1$ operations create the same connected components as adding every pair. Two elements are circuit-connected precisely when they have the same final representative. A weight belongs to $L(M)$ precisely when it is constant in every resulting class. With path compression and union by rank, the time is $O((n+L)\alpha(n))$ and space is $O(n)$.

\begin{example}[Overlapping circuits]
Let
\[
E=\{0,1,2,3\},\qquad \cC=\{\{0,1,2\},\{1,2,3\}\}.
\]
The circuits overlap, so all four elements form one component. The weight $w=(0,0,2,0)$ passes both tests: the first circuit is minimized at $0,1$, and the second at $1,3$. Every $w+c\bone$ also passes. A lineality weight must be constant, since the first circuit gives $v_0=v_1=v_2$ and the second gives $v_1=v_2=v_3$.
\end{example}

For floating-point data, equality may be replaced operationally by a declared tolerance $\varepsilon$, counting values within $\varepsilon$ of the minimum. This is an approximate test rather than the exact predicate and must be interpreted carefully near boundaries.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

For a graphic matroid, circuits are simple cycles. A weight is in the Bergman fan exactly when no cycle has a unique minimum-weight edge. Circuit components identify edge groups linked through cycles. For represented matroids, each circuit supports a minimal linear dependence among columns, and the corresponding tropical equation requires at least two least coordinates on that support.

Translation invariance permits projective normalization. For finite nonempty $E$, one may subtract $\min_{e\in E}w_e$ so the smallest coordinate is zero, or fix one chosen coordinate to zero. Such normalization changes no circuit test. The orbit theorem explains why the quotient is natural: each common-translation class is a path-connected affine orbit wholly contained in the fan.

The component theorem interprets connectivity as a symmetry count. A connected circuit system admits one common lineality constant. Several components admit distinct constants because no circuit compares values across components. This points toward a finite-dimensional formula equating lineality dimension with component count.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

Several distinctions are essential. Common translation is not arbitrary translation; a nonconstant shift can alter circuit minimizers. Negative dilation reverses inequalities and need not preserve minima. The full fan need not be ordinarily convex even though each translation orbit is convex. Nestedness has been defined and its cone corollary established, but stronger nested convexity requires additional work.

Natural directions include the following.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item Prove, for finite loopless matroids, that lineality dimension equals the number of circuit components.
\item Determine whether the projectivized Bergman fan of every finite circuit-connected positive-rank matroid is path connected.
\item Determine whether nested matroids have ordinarily convex polyhedral projectivized Bergman fans.
\item Characterize circuit-generating families, closed under suitable elimination, whose repeated-minimum equations cut out the full fan.
\item Establish a product description for Bergman fans of direct sums, compatible with circuit components and lineality.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The Bergman fan and the tropical linear space of the circuit ideal are the same set because both enforce one rule: every circuit minimum must occur at least twice. This rule survives common translation and nonnegative dilation. Circuitwise-constant weights are exactly componentwise-constant weights, so circuit connectivity reduces lineality to constants. Every common-translation orbit is convex and path connected and, through a Bergman weight, stays entirely inside the fan. Circuits thus supply the equations, their overlap supplies the components, and tied minima supply both symmetry and topology.

